 "findings": [
  {
   "evidence": "The statement requires `hS : (S \u2229 closedBall c R).Finite` and concludes `r \u2264 R`. The docstring reads 'If a point strictly inside a closed ball is closer to the centre than every other point of `S` in that ball, a smaller open ball intersects `S` in that point alone.'",
   "file": "TauCeti/Topology/MetricSpace/NearestPoint.lean",
   "fix": "Reword, e.g.: 'If `S` has finitely many points in `closedBall c R` and `p \u2208 S` lies strictly inside that ball and strictly closer to `c` than every other such point, then some open ball `ball c r` with `dist p c < r \u2264 R` meets `S` only in `p`.'",
   "issue": "The docstring says strict nearest-point minimality alone gives an isolating ball. It leaves out the hypothesis that `S \u2229 closedBall c R` is finite, and without it the claim is false (the PR's own counterexample: `S = [1,2]`, `c = 0`, `p = 1`, `R = 2`). It also says 'a smaller open ball', but the theorem only gives `r \u2264 R`, and `r = R` when there are no competitors.",
   "line": 23
  }
 ],
